92: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
93:     """Copy the existing foreground sprite into every three-marker corner frame, reflecting it to the frame's missing corner.
94: 
95:     Input structure
96:     - The background is the dominant color.
97:     - One non-background color forms singleton marker pixels; every three markers are three corners of a same-size square.
98:     - The other non-background color forms one sprite mask already placed at the missing corner of one marker square.
99: 
100:     Rule
101:     - Find the source marker square whose missing corner touches the corresponding corner of the sprite bbox.
102:     - For every marker square with the same side length, place a copy of that sprite outside the square at its missing corner.
103:     - Reflect the sprite vertically and/or horizontally when the target missing corner lies in a different quadrant from the source missing corner.
104:     - If the source bbox is a complete hollow square border, keep the corner-bracket motif selected by the source anchor.
105: 
106:     Key insight
107:     - The yellow three-corner squares are control frames: their absent fourth corner gives both the anchor point and the quadrant for the red sprite copy.
108:     """
109:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
110:     bg = get_bg(g)
111:     colors = colors_present(g, exclude={bg})
112:     assert len(colors) == 2
113: 
114:     singleton_colors = [
115:         color
116:         for color in colors
117:         if all(len(component) == 1 for component in components_mask(g == color, connectivity=8))
118:     ]
